WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:06.990
Smarter than a beaver and as heroic as Schwarzenegger,
Naru from "Prey" has plenty of amazing moments

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:10.260
— but which ones are the best and bravest?

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.050
Right away in the first five minutes of "Prey,"
we're given a telling glimpse into the kind

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:16.529
of character Naru will prove herself to be.

00:00:16.529 --> 00:00:21.679
At the start of the movie, Naru's trusty dog
Sarrii gets her tail stuck in a bear trap.

00:00:21.679 --> 00:00:25.429
Not only does this foreshadow later scenes
in which bear traps will appear in much more

00:00:25.429 --> 00:00:30.119
dire circumstances, it also says a lot about
Naru without any dialogue at all.

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:34.469
By saving her dog, the first thing we get
to know about Naru is her compassion and her

00:00:34.469 --> 00:00:37.010
utter fearlessness when a loved one is hurt.

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:41.329
Despite her struggles with them, she values
her family and will protect them at all costs.

00:00:41.329 --> 00:00:44.739
However, this isn't all there is to the movie's
young Comanche hero.

00:00:44.739 --> 00:00:50.540
She is, above all, incredibly driven to prove
herself as a hunter, despite the gender norms of her tribe.

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:53.780
This drive will go on to inspire her actions
throughout "Prey."

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:56.720
"Why do you want to hunt?"

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:59.120
"Because you all think that I can't."

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:03.289
Director Dan Trachtenberg and his crew clearly
recognized their obligation to bring some

00:01:03.289 --> 00:01:09.389
sweet new gadgets for the Predator to use,
and "Prey" delivers spectacularly on this front.

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:12.760
However, one of the coolest pieces of tech
in the film is actually developed by Naru.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:17.160
Early on into hunting the alien, she ties
a rope to her hatchet in order to be able

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.550
to throw and retrieve the weapon quickly.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:23.420
Trachtenberg revealed during an interview
with ComicBook.com that he drew inspiration

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:28.660
from the 2018 "God of War" video game for
this rope ax that allows Naru to pull off

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:32.270
some stylish kills without ever losing her
trusty weapon.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:36.470
It goes on to pay off with a crucial role
in the movie, particularly in the scene when

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:41.380
Naru first stumbles into the quicksand swamp
and finds herself sinking with seemingly little

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:42.540
hope of escape.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:48.253
The only way out is to use the rope ax to
latch onto a downed tree and pull herself up.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:51.740
This moment highlights Naru's resourcefulness
and her ability to think on her feet — a

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:56.340
unique form of bravery that will prove to
be important later on.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.210
After attacking the Comanche hunting party
in the bloody scene prior, the Predator stalks

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:03.910
Naru and Itsee into the tall grass clearing
near the camp.

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:08.391
Hiding out in the grass, Itsee, an overconfident
hunter in the Comanche tribe, spots the Predator

00:02:08.391 --> 00:02:12.160
and prepares his bow, thinking that he and
Naru have the upper hand.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.460
"We have him."

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.530
"No, he has us."

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:22.350
Naru saves Itsee from being shot, but as the
two run away, Itsee is caught by the Predator

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.750
and dies in one of the most brutal deaths
"Prey" has to offer.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:29.480
This moment illustrates that although she
hasn't gotten the chance to prove herself,

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:34.280
Naru truly is a skilled hunter who uses her
instincts to assess any kind of situation.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:38.780
She has the wisdom to recognize that choosing
what keeps you alive in a long battle of attrition

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:42.980
with a foe like the Predator is always the
correct choice.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:47.700
After the Predator's assault on the French
trappers' camp, Naru returns to retrieve Sarii,

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.340
but she's surprised to find that one of the
men still clings to life.

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:54.510
The survivor turns out to be the group's translator,
Raphael.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.280
He begs Naru to heal him before he bleeds
out.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.390
However, taking advantage of her position
of power, she will only do it if Raphael teaches

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:03.760
her how to use his Flintlock pistol.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:07.760
Barely clinging to life, the translator has
no choice but to agree to her terms.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.720
After demonstrating how it works, he gives
her the Flintlock and Naru gives him medicinal

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:13.900
herbs to treat his bleeding wound.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:18.200
After she uses the herbs on Raphael, the Predator
returns, allowing Naru to discover a hidden

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:19.770
property of her medicine.

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:23.950
The Predator can't see Raphael due to the
medicine reducing his body heat, and that's

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:28.020
when Naru realizes that its vision is based
on heat signatures.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:31.680
This moment is, of course, a big reference
to the original "Predator," echoing the scene

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:36.040
in which Arnold Schwarzenegger's Dutch discovers
that the Predator can't see him when he's

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:37.770
covered in mud.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:42.319
As sharp as she may be, Naru really rides
the line between being brave and being an

00:03:42.319 --> 00:03:45.959
idiot when she decides to leave the camp without
telling anyone where she's headed.

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:49.560
In pursuit of the Predator, Naru is getting
in way over her head.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:53.680
Ultimately though, this proves to be an act
of bravery instead of foolishness, and ending

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.130
up as a real turning point in "Prey."

00:03:56.130 --> 00:04:00.870
Naru needs to embark on her "kühtaamia,"
a rite of passage that all hunters must undergo

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:02.910
in order to prove themselves to the tribe.

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:07.180
The details of this ritual are important,
since they require the hunter to hunt an animal

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:09.849
that can also hunt them — a predator.

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:14.870
Naru realizes before everyone else that the
alien interloper is a hunter, and she accepts

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:18.219
his arrival as a sign it is her time to be
tested.

00:04:18.219 --> 00:04:22.060
The moment she decides to go out on her own,
without anyone's help, Naru commits to making

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:26.440
this hunt her kühtaamia, and that drive to
prove herself to everyone inspires her for

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:28.169
the rest of the movie.

00:04:28.169 --> 00:04:31.949
At the end of the movie, Naru gets to complete
her arc in a triumphant finale.

00:04:31.949 --> 00:04:36.240
As she returns from the hunt victorious, she
comes back to the camp covered in neon green

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.569
Predator blood with the head of her prey held
in her hands.

00:04:39.569 --> 00:04:44.259
It's the most empowering moment Naru gets
in the whole movie, bringing her character full circle.

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:48.020
As the community gathers around her, she tosses
the head at the feet of the tribe's leader

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:50.949
to accept her rightful place as a Comanche
warrior.

00:04:50.949 --> 00:04:55.520
This isn't merely Naru coming back from her
kühtaamia, regularly a cause for celebration,

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:58.410
but her returning as the savior of her tribe.

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:02.759
Having killed the creature that took her brother
and most of the male hunters, Naru has made

00:05:02.759 --> 00:05:05.849
her way to the top of the food chain in a
short matter of time.

00:05:05.849 --> 00:05:10.759
With most of the hunters dead, Naru remains
the singular defense the tribe has.

00:05:10.759 --> 00:05:14.789
After they're both independently captured
by the French trappers, Naru and Taabe are

00:05:14.789 --> 00:05:18.199
tied up to a tree together and used as bait
for the Predator.

00:05:18.199 --> 00:05:22.479
But since the Predator only hunts humans and
animals who pose a threat to it, it ignores

00:05:22.479 --> 00:05:27.069
the captives and goes straight for the armed
trappers who are waiting in the wings to attack.

00:05:27.069 --> 00:05:32.263
Amid the chaos, Naru thinks on her feet to
figure out how to free herself and Taabe from their ropes.

00:05:32.289 --> 00:05:35.930
Upon seeing the bear trap on the group near
their restraints, Naru figures out how she

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:37.509
can use it to cut their ropes.

00:05:37.509 --> 00:05:41.819
Trachtenberg uses this opportunity to trick
the viewer, as Naru begins telling Taabe a

00:05:41.819 --> 00:05:45.979
story about how a beaver will escape from
traps by chewing its own leg off.

00:05:45.979 --> 00:05:50.540
Taabe begins to panic, thinking his sister
is about to sacrifice her limb for their freedom.

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:55.371
But as we've learned, Naru always thinks past
the obvious answer to find a better one.

00:05:55.371 --> 00:05:56.249
"Naru stop!"

00:06:00.198 --> 00:06:02.207
"...I'm smarter than a beaver."

00:06:02.207 --> 00:06:07.958
One of the most brutal moments in "Prey" doesn't
even need a Predator around to set off a flurry of violence.

00:06:07.979 --> 00:06:12.250
Instead, in a moment showing the parallels
between our young hero and the movie's young

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:14.869
Predator, Naru is the instigator of this assault.

00:06:14.869 --> 00:06:20.501
When sneaking up on the trapper camp, she
sees that they have captured Sarii and intend to harm him.

00:06:20.529 --> 00:06:23.569
When she springs into action, Naru is quick
and deadly.

00:06:23.569 --> 00:06:27.939
Using her assailants' weapons against them,
as well as her own rope ax, she swiftly puts

00:06:27.939 --> 00:06:30.059
an end to the remaining trappers.

00:06:30.059 --> 00:06:33.720
It's the moment that shows the most violent
side of her, but it is crucial to proving

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:35.749
that she's ready to take on the Predator.

00:06:35.749 --> 00:06:40.150
Only now that as she matches the efficiency,
bravery, and brutality of her prey can she

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:42.580
understand how to take it down.

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:46.979
According to The Escapist, one of the elements
of the original "Predator" that Dan Trachtenberg

00:06:46.979 --> 00:06:51.330
picked up and amplified in "Prey" is its commentary
on colonialism.

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:55.971
The anti-colonial themes are summed up in
the antagonistic relationship between the

00:06:55.971 --> 00:06:57.689
Comanche and the French trappers.

00:06:57.689 --> 00:07:02.668
The movie becomes as much about Naru facing
off against them as it does about her hunt for the Predator.

00:07:02.669 --> 00:07:07.210
In the end, the final trapper's fate is the
most brutal of them all — but the elaborate

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:12.330
plan that Naru uses to kill two birds with
one stone shines as one of her bravest moments.

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:15.967
"You can't see, but I'm killing you."

00:07:15.967 --> 00:07:20.180
After we see Naru sneaking up on the trapper,
we see that she has tied him up in an elaborate

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:21.830
setup to lure the Predator out.

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:26.289
She's cut off his leg below the knee, bleeding
him in order to attract the alien while hiding

00:07:26.289 --> 00:07:29.279
herself to its gaze using the medicinal herbs.

00:07:29.279 --> 00:07:33.310
Needless to say, it's incredibly dangerous
to attract a Predator to you, but this is

00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:38.220
the type of bravery Naru needs as a hunter
to complete her rite of passage.

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:42.520
At the end of "Prey," Naru gets to put her
bravery to the test in one final face-off

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:43.680
with the Predator.

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:47.960
After luring her prey into the Frenchman-baited
trap, Naru begins to use her wits and tools

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:50.309
to prove herself as the true predator.

00:07:50.309 --> 00:07:55.276
The first and most important part of the plan
is to shoot the Predator point blank in the head.

00:07:55.289 --> 00:07:58.849
Not only does this strategy inflict a major
head wound to her foe, but it ends up being

00:07:58.849 --> 00:08:01.069
crucial to how the fight plays out.

00:08:01.069 --> 00:08:04.590
When Naru shoots the Predator with the Flintlock,
his mask flies off.

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:09.754
Earlier, Naru had observed that this mask
controls the targeting on the Predator's spear gun.

00:08:09.759 --> 00:08:13.840
She knows he is weaker without it, so she
cleverly uses the element of surprise to seriously

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:15.789
handicap her enemy.

00:08:15.789 --> 00:08:19.439
As you watch "Prey," it becomes clear that
many moments of Naru's final fight have been

00:08:19.439 --> 00:08:24.933
plotted out in advance, but one of the best
scenes in the sequence comes when she's forced to improvise.

00:08:24.939 --> 00:08:29.149
Near the swamp trap, Naru is forced between
two rocks by the Predator, with its shield

00:08:29.149 --> 00:08:31.520
coming down slowly, threatening to decapitate
her.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:36.000
With the Predator bearing down on her, Naru
grabs a hold of the mandible horn coming out

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:37.430
from its mouth area.

00:08:37.430 --> 00:08:41.159
She uses it to stab the Predator in the face,
and takes advantage of the moment when the

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:42.949
beast cries out in pain to flee.

00:08:42.949 --> 00:08:48.518
It's a small beat in the middle of this awesome
fight, but it stands out due to its sheer brutality.

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:52.720
The elaborate and ingenious trap that Naru
sets up to finally take down the Predator

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:54.800
is definitely the coolest moment in "Prey."

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.910
The film keeps you on your toes throughout
the entire climatic battle, only to reveal

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:03.690
at the moment of the final kill that Naru
has had everything in the bag the whole time.

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:06.960
From the start of the sequence when she's
hunting the trapper, Naru has been setting

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:08.540
her plan into motion.

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:12.690
Immediately after she took the shot, she grabbed
the mask so she could later put it in its

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:15.089
strategic place in the tree near the swamp.

00:09:15.089 --> 00:09:19.230
When she finally lures the Predator into the
swamp, she has the mask's targeting lasers

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:20.680
aimed right at it.

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:25.350
When it attempts to fire its spears at Naru,
the projectiles miss and hone in on the Predator

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:27.210
instead, killing it immediately.

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:30.720
In her victorious moment, Naru reminds us
that killing the Predator was never about

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:34.430
firepower – it was about embracing the traits
of a good hunter.

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:38.650
Patience, cunning, and bravery all help Naru
defeat the Predator with its own equipment.

